WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.430
Hi, this is Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.850
Uh, today I want to talk
about how I learned Ukrainian.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.880
Now, I have been doing most of my videos
in my little studio, but today I'm going

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.860
to do it in front of this painting.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.270
And I'm going to tell you why,
but first of all, if you enjoy my

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:20.790
videos, please subscribe, click
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.490
And if you follow me on a, a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:30.450
So before I get into talking about how I
learned Ukrainian, I'll explain why I'm

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.970
doing it in front of this painting here.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.790
This is a print, of course
it's not an original.

00:00:36.089 --> 00:00:38.610
It is uh...

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.660
[The Cafe Terrace on the Place du Forum].

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.280
Painted by...

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:52.379
painted by van Gogh, van Gogh in English,
the famous Dutch impressionist painter.

00:00:53.205 --> 00:00:57.525
And I like doing it in front of this
picture because it has the Ukrainian

00:00:57.945 --> 00:01:04.394
colors, the blue and the yellow plus I
very much liked this print and, you know,

00:01:04.425 --> 00:01:07.155
van Gogh, van Gogh was a Dutch painter.

00:01:07.185 --> 00:01:11.625
He spent a lot of time in France,
Southern France, also in Normandy.

00:01:12.315 --> 00:01:16.095
And as someone that is interested in
languages, you won't be surprised to

00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:20.985
know that I kind of like the mixture
of cultures and even in food, you

00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:27.660
know, Olive oil with my sushi or a
wasabi with my French food or whatever.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:28.860
I like mixing things.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.000
And similarly, I like mixing
cultures and I like the way one

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.950
culture leads you to another.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.610
Uh, and by exploring all these
different cultures, you learn

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.440
more and more about the world.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.460
And my discovery of Ukrainian
and Ukraine is a good example.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:51.180
So, for most of my life, I knew
next to nothing about Ukraine.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.420
I think like a lot of people, I
thought it was some kind of appendage

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.350
to Russia, similar to Russia.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.780
And I didn't know much more than that.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.000
Uh, in 2000 and I would say six or
seven, I started learning Russian,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.830
uh, largely to see whether I could
learn a Slavic language with all

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.864
the complicated grammar uh, without
spending a lot of time on grammar.

00:02:14.894 --> 00:02:15.644
So that was...

00:02:15.674 --> 00:02:20.595
that plus having read, you know,
Tolstoy and Dostoevsky in my late

00:02:20.595 --> 00:02:23.774
teens, I thought it would be cool
to read it in the original Russian.

00:02:24.495 --> 00:02:28.964
So I was with Russian, learning Russian,
enjoying Russian, listening to Russian

00:02:28.964 --> 00:02:35.355
audio books, reading on LingQ and, um,
of course in 2008 there was the war

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:38.655
in Georgia, which I followed because
I followed Ekho Moskvy much to the

00:02:38.655 --> 00:02:42.570
disappointment of many Russians who
are caught up in the sort of propaganda

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.920
world that has been created in Russia...

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.820
They're the only good people in the
world, everybody else is mean to them.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.070
And Ekho Moskvy was kind of
tolerated as sort of a safety

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.230
valve for a variety of opinions.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:01.560
A lot of these opinions were critical
of the government, but you also had the

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.585
communist party would be interviewed and
the, you know, whatever his name was.

00:03:06.645 --> 00:03:07.325
I can't remember...

00:03:07.355 --> 00:03:15.045
can't remember and, um, extreme,
uh, imperialists types, like, uh, I

00:03:15.045 --> 00:03:18.575
think it's a Prokhanov was on there.

00:03:19.545 --> 00:03:21.855
At any rate, so Russia invaded Georgia.

00:03:22.725 --> 00:03:23.925
I didn't pay much attention.

00:03:24.615 --> 00:03:28.815
2010, there was an election
in Ukraine and Yanukovych won.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.920
And of course the Russians were
following that very closely,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.930
but still it was all far away.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:34.980
I didn't pay much attention.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:43.140
Then in 2014, Russia took Crimea
and I watched that and somebody

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.400
told me that I could follow these
events on Ukrainian television.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:53.220
So I started watching a program
called Shuster Live and lo and behold,

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.195
on Shuster Live half the people
speak Russian or spoke Russian,

00:03:57.375 --> 00:03:59.144
half the people spoke Ukrainian.

00:03:59.595 --> 00:04:03.734
And as I was listening to the
Ukrainian, I felt as though I

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:06.225
should be able to understand it,
but I couldn't understand it.

00:04:06.435 --> 00:04:11.265
Very strange feeling because it's
pronounced in many ways, kind of

00:04:11.265 --> 00:04:14.924
sounds like Russian, but in fact,
so many of the words are different.

00:04:16.034 --> 00:04:20.364
So, uh, I decided that
I would learn Ukrainian.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:24.270
And, uh, the, I bought some books on...

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:31.290
Starter books and also a radio free
Europe, uh, out of Prague, uh, was

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:39.240
putting out Rádio Svobodná  in Ukrainian
put out regular interviews, um,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.820
where they had both audio and text.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.830
So knowing Russian and, and what I
discovered was that like half the

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:51.285
words are like 60% of the words are
like Russian, and so if I had, you

00:04:51.285 --> 00:04:55.335
know, audio material with transcript,
then I could import that into LingQ.

00:04:56.415 --> 00:04:59.145
And very quickly I
could acquire new words.

00:04:59.145 --> 00:05:02.595
I think my vocabulary count on
LingQ in Ukrainian is like 50 or

00:05:02.595 --> 00:05:06.585
60,000 known words, because so many
words you can guess what they mean.

00:05:07.395 --> 00:05:08.595
A few of you have to learn.

00:05:09.045 --> 00:05:09.885
And I just listened.

00:05:09.915 --> 00:05:10.875
I can imagine...

00:05:10.875 --> 00:05:15.875
I think I've read one and a half
million words of Ukrainian on LingQ.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.090
I don't know how many
hours I've listened to.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:22.950
In fact, I'll show you, uh, in fact,
let me show you my profile in Russian,

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:27.690
which shows that I began around 2007, and
then you'll see my profile on Ukrainian,

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.750
which shows that I began around 2014.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.159
And you can see where I have been
most active in those languages.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.309
So listening to all this stuff
and also Hromadske Radio which

00:05:40.309 --> 00:05:47.400
is a very good source of audio of
podcasts, but without transcript.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.020
So it was only after training myself
on the Radio Free Europe material

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.580
that I was good enough to understand
a lot of the podcasts on Hromadske

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.280
Radio, most of which were in Ukrainian,
some of which were in Russian.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:07.170
So I was able to follow the events in
the Donbas, but let's go back to Crimea.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:08.760
So I followed the events there.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:15.210
Uh, initially Russia claimed that
the little green men with no military

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.150
insignia were not their soldiers,
which of course was not true.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:21.660
Uh, Putin subsequently
admitted that yes...

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.980
you know, at first he
said, oh, they just...

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:28.830
Those are just people who went and bought
military, uh, uniforms at some store.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:34.230
Uh, and then he admitted that they were
in fact Russian soldiers and the events

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:42.550
in Crimea were planned by a group of
agents who subsequently went to the Donbas

00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:47.729
and took what was a, quite a volatile
situation and turned it into a war.

00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:50.760
And I followed this, uh, daily.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.460
Uh, I followed it as, uh, you know, the,
the people in the Donbas and stepping

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.099
back obviously there was a lot of tension.

00:06:59.099 --> 00:07:01.700
You could see this at Shuster Live.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:05.010
You had the representatives of
the sort of pro Russian faction.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:11.365
You had the uh, the, the right wing, more
nationalistic types, like the head of

00:07:11.605 --> 00:07:18.585
Svoboda you had this guy ... who is a bit
of a nutcase, also a right wing extremist.

00:07:18.975 --> 00:07:22.035
You had everybody, and you had people
who are subsequently, subsequently

00:07:22.035 --> 00:07:23.535
identified as being corrupt.

00:07:23.775 --> 00:07:27.615
You know, you had the sense of, of Ukraine
is full of these people who are very

00:07:27.885 --> 00:07:32.325
eloquent and speak with great passion
and a tremendous range of opinions,

00:07:32.505 --> 00:07:36.600
and then Shuster Live as they speak
the people in the audience vote on what

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.040
they think of what they're saying there.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:43.140
So it was obviously a period of
tremendous tension because of the

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:49.290
events of the maidan which have
brought in, uh, a government, which

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:53.400
was voted by all parties, by the
way, uh, which was more pro Western.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:58.500
It was more sort of pro
nationalist, uh, than the previous

00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.640
government under Yanukovych.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.770
And there were people in Eastern Ukraine.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.195
who didn't want that.

00:08:03.224 --> 00:08:06.705
These are people who had voted
for Yanukovych, they're guy fled.

00:08:06.974 --> 00:08:08.594
So obviously they're upset.

00:08:08.925 --> 00:08:13.414
I'm watching this in Ukrainian
every day, following it.

00:08:13.955 --> 00:08:20.220
If I get my, a podcast from, uh,
Radio Svoboda, I've now got a text.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:22.650
So I'm very interested in the subject.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:29.470
I'm following it as a, you know, Girkin is
the first minister of war for the Dunbas.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.220
They call themselves The
Donetsk People's Republic.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:37.289
Borodai Is the prime minister of
The Donetsk People's Republic.

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:42.825
There's a guy called Bessler who is
some kind of a sleeping russian GRU

00:08:42.825 --> 00:08:47.625
agent that's been living there and
he organizes, you know, uh, militia.

00:08:47.895 --> 00:08:51.285
And so they go from storming a
government buildings to eventually

00:08:51.285 --> 00:08:52.905
having some kind of an armed force.

00:08:53.055 --> 00:08:53.865
There was a war.

00:08:54.975 --> 00:08:59.565
Uh, Ukrainian army is very poorly
organized between the Ukrainian

00:08:59.565 --> 00:09:04.785
army and volunteers, largely from
Eastern Ukraine, they fight back.

00:09:04.785 --> 00:09:10.650
They were able to eventually arrive at
a sort of a cease fire line after the

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:15.000
intervention of the Russian regular
Russian army to prevent the Ukrainian army

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.550
from actually taking back that whole area.

00:09:17.939 --> 00:09:21.780
There were people killed on both
sides, mostly soldiers, Ukrainian

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:26.910
soldiers, Ukrainian government soldiers,
volunteers on the Ukrainian side, the

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:31.460
uh separatist fighters on the other
side, Russian soldiers from Ossetia

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:35.520
or wherever they came, Yakutia, there
was quite a few sort of Mongolian

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.610
types there who were, um, shown...

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:43.290
you know, some of them were in hospital
with serious, with serious burns.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:45.870
It's a very unhappy situation.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.740
A lot of people fled, displaced
people to other parts of Ukraine,

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:54.839
some fled to Russia, a total death
toll is supposedly around 15,000.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:56.459
I followed it every day.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.030
And so with that my Ukrainian
comprehension improved.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:06.795
Now, uh, I, at some point I said, I
should start speaking Ukrainian, very

00:10:06.795 --> 00:10:10.785
difficult to do because I'm so used to
speaking Russian, but I had a Russian

00:10:10.785 --> 00:10:12.255
tutor who was actually from Ukraine.

00:10:12.645 --> 00:10:16.155
And so slowly, slowly, I started
speaking Ukrainian with her.

00:10:16.455 --> 00:10:19.755
She subsequently immigrated to the United
States with her husband and family.

00:10:20.235 --> 00:10:25.795
Uh, then I found two more tutors,
one in Lviv and one in Dnipro.

00:10:26.075 --> 00:10:32.880
Uh, the one in Dnipro eventually at some
point, uh, with all of the shelling going

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.220
on back and forth across the demarcation
line and people living, uh, without

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.860
gas, without water, I said, I want
to do something to help those people.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.860
Uh, my, uh, Ukrainian tutor
in Dnipro introduced me to

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.700
this small non-governmental
organization in a ...which is a

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:53.569
suburb of the city of Donetsk.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:58.470
And every quarter I sent the money
so that they could buy, um, you

00:10:58.470 --> 00:10:59.960
know, diapers for old people.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.170
We built, uh, kitchens for school,
for a school, a little park.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.170
They did a whole bunch of stuff to improve
the quality of life of those people.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:11.295
I eventually did visit,
was very well received.

00:11:11.295 --> 00:11:14.805
Kids were singing and dancing
and, uh, I got the bread and salt

00:11:14.834 --> 00:11:18.525
treatment and, uh, and most people
in Eastern Ukraine speak Russian,

00:11:18.555 --> 00:11:20.025
but some of them do speak Ukrainian.

00:11:21.045 --> 00:11:22.094
So I went there.

00:11:22.305 --> 00:11:26.535
I was also in Kiev and then I was
in Lviv, which is a wonderful city.

00:11:26.714 --> 00:11:30.464
And again, when we're talking about
the sort of connections of languages

00:11:30.464 --> 00:11:37.604
and peoples one of the other aside from
Radio Svoboda, another major source

00:11:37.604 --> 00:11:42.915
of my, uh, listening and reading in
Ukrainian was the history of Ukraine.

00:11:43.545 --> 00:11:49.365
And I have a number of these,
um, audio books, eBooks, I in

00:11:49.365 --> 00:11:53.145
particular liked lay on it, uh, Leonid Zalizniak.

00:11:53.984 --> 00:11:57.555
And so I listened and listened to
listen to and read in Ukrainian.

00:11:57.555 --> 00:12:04.469
I discovered lo and behold, what I didn't
realize that Ukraine had ahistory which

00:12:04.469 --> 00:12:09.959
involved the original ... which involved
being invaded by the Mongols, which

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:15.569
then involved them being conquered by
the Lithuanians who are welcomed by the

00:12:15.569 --> 00:12:21.300
Ukrainians because they were pushing back
against the Mongols, uh, that eventually

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:26.709
become becomes the Lithuanian, Polish
or Polish Lithuanian Commonwealth.

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:31.155
The Poles, uh, but, but there's a
whole history of, of interaction

00:12:31.155 --> 00:12:32.385
between Ukrainians and Poles.

00:12:32.685 --> 00:12:36.705
And so that the vocabulary in
Ukrainian is closer to Polish.

00:12:36.795 --> 00:12:40.545
I don't know if the Poles influenced
the Ukrainian language or the Ukrainians

00:12:40.545 --> 00:12:44.505
influenced the Polish language, or they
all came from some church Slavonic.

00:12:44.505 --> 00:12:48.285
I have no idea, but the fact is
that from a vocabulary point of

00:12:48.285 --> 00:12:54.375
view, Russian is a bit of an outlier
and the Ukrainian and Polish and

00:12:54.375 --> 00:12:56.265
even Czech vocabulary are closer.

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:00.000
All of a sudden I began seeing...

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:04.020
you know, and then you have, uh,
the Poles were kind of trying to

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:08.370
force the Ukrainians to become
Catholic, the Ukrainians resisted.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:11.800
And there was a whole bunch of
unpleasantness resulting eventually

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:16.590
in the Khmelnytsky rebellion where
basically everything to the east of

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:21.090
the Dnieper River went over to Russia
with a promise of some independents,

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:22.260
which turned out not to be the case.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:28.860
I only mentioned this because my learning
of Ukrainian involved following the

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:34.260
day-to-day events in Ukraine, and also
learning a great deal about the history.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:39.280
And so then when I visited
Lviv, so here you are in Lviv.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:44.850
Again, if we talk about fusion of
cultures and so forth Lviv before the

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:51.420
war, before the second world war was
50% Polish, 30% Jewish, 20% Ukrainian

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:57.435
because the Ukrainians speakers lived
in the countryside and Lviv, which was

00:13:57.435 --> 00:14:03.435
known to the Germans as Lemberg and to
the Poles as Lwów was the fourth largest

00:14:03.435 --> 00:14:05.625
city in the Austro-Hungarian empire.

00:14:05.984 --> 00:14:12.495
And so you see in, Lviv the sort of
evidence of these different national

00:14:12.495 --> 00:14:17.775
historical religious, you know, buildings
there's even an Armenian church there.

00:14:18.375 --> 00:14:21.495
Uh, and so again, it's this
sort of blending of cultures.

00:14:22.875 --> 00:14:26.354
Uh, another thing that I, I firmly
believe is that the, our culture

00:14:26.354 --> 00:14:28.275
and our language is not in our DNA.

00:14:28.814 --> 00:14:32.925
So there are lots of people in Russia
who have Ukrainian family names.

00:14:32.954 --> 00:14:34.635
That doesn't mean that they're Ukrainian.

00:14:35.055 --> 00:14:38.685
There are lots of people in Ukraine
who have Russian family names who

00:14:38.685 --> 00:14:43.064
may even have Russian parents or
grandparents who may even speak Russian.

00:14:43.064 --> 00:14:47.834
In fact, half the Ukrainians speak Russian
more often than they speak Ukrainian.

00:14:47.864 --> 00:14:50.714
From what I can gather, whether
it's 50, 50, 60, 40, I don't know.

00:14:51.194 --> 00:14:53.640
But those people identify as Ukrainians.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:56.400
So the DNA is not the issue.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:57.990
It's, it's what you want to be.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:14:58.920
That is the issue.

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:04.740
And that's kind of part of the problem
right now that the Russians are not

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:09.599
prepared to accept that regardless
of the DNA of individual Ukrainians,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:15.240
they, in their majority, not all,
majority want to identify as Ukrainian,

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:19.890
want to have a separate existence as
Ukrainian and their language as well.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:24.569
And I've never believed that there are
great languages and small languages.

00:15:24.569 --> 00:15:26.200
Like after Russian I learned Czech.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:29.850
Czech has 10 million speakers, Russian
does a hundred and who knows...

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:30.930
hundred and 80 million speakers.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:31.620
It doesn't matter.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:35.280
Um, every language is wonderful.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:35.970
It's beautiful.

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:40.350
There is no more beautiful language, more
important language, greater language.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.300
It's whatever you want to learn.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:46.590
And as you learn that you discover
a whole bunch of things about that

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:49.500
language, culture, place, history
that you didn't know before.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:53.640
That's been my experience with every
language that I have learned, and I'm

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:57.480
discovering it now with Arabic and
Persian and a little bit of Turkish.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:02.940
So, uh, you know, it's a little bit
of a disjointed rant here following

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:07.770
up on the video where I said we're
offering free Ukrainian on LingQ to

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:12.010
anyone who wants to learn Ukrainian and
free access to LingQ to all Ukrainian

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.460
refugees or residents in Ukraine.

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:18.870
I should point out that that
offer is valid for all refugees.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:24.610
Uh, so I'm hoping that Syrians and
Afghans will, uh, take advantage.

00:16:25.605 --> 00:16:29.825
Uh, as of today, I think we have 3,600
Ukrainians who have approached support

00:16:29.835 --> 00:16:31.945
at LingQ to get free  access to LingQ.

00:16:33.015 --> 00:16:35.475
I'm hoping that other
refugees will also do that.

00:16:35.805 --> 00:16:41.305
And we're going to produce tutorials,
uh, in Ukrainian and also Sahra at

00:16:41.305 --> 00:16:48.345
LingQ can help to provide Farsi,
um, version of these tutorials for

00:16:48.345 --> 00:16:49.895
Afghan refugees who speak Farsi.

00:16:50.815 --> 00:16:53.955
And hopefully we can do the same in
Syrian Arabic, or even in standard

00:16:53.955 --> 00:16:59.025
Arabic and in Pashto, if we can find
people to help us with those versions.

00:16:59.985 --> 00:17:02.655
So a bit, again, I tend
to wander on these.

00:17:02.655 --> 00:17:07.395
I hope that's of interest, a little
bit long maybe, but, um, that's

00:17:07.395 --> 00:17:09.645
how I got involved with Ukrainian.

00:17:10.035 --> 00:17:12.745
I probably forgotten if
you, oh, I should...yes.

00:17:13.215 --> 00:17:17.565
One of the things I did when I went
to Lviv, where I met with my online

00:17:17.565 --> 00:17:22.970
tutor Solomia and we set up a system
whereby she took me around Lviv.

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:28.830
We visited churches and cafes and
cemeteries and all different things

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:34.170
that, you know, monasteries and we
would just speak Ukrainian and she would

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:40.140
type up in her little cell phone, all
of the errors that I made in terms of

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:42.870
know of usage and phrases and words.

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:45.870
And she would then send
them to me by email.

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:48.720
I would import that
into LingQ and study it.

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.410
So we did that from 9-12.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:55.060
I go back to my room, I study it,
go back to two o'clock and 2-5.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:56.310
And we did that for five days.

00:17:56.490 --> 00:18:00.780
And that is a tremendous way to
discover the city of Lviv and

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:02.550
also to work on my Ukrainian.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:04.260
Uh, so I forgot about that.

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:06.300
Uh, something I would recommend.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:09.420
In fact, when I was in Kyiv, I was
thinking, boy, it would be great to be

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:13.875
able to connect with a tutor and say,
look, I want you to take me around Kyiv.

00:18:14.265 --> 00:18:17.685
We're going to speak Ukrainian or Russian,
whatever the case may be, three hours

00:18:17.685 --> 00:18:19.245
in the morning, maybe just morning.

00:18:19.755 --> 00:18:22.995
And then I can study all of that
and we go back to it the next day

00:18:22.995 --> 00:18:23.985
and the next and the next day.

00:18:23.985 --> 00:18:28.485
So I want you to do that in Italian
and Spanish or Chinese or Japanese,

00:18:28.485 --> 00:18:33.705
wherever you are, and sort of integrating
that one-on-one contact with a tutor.

00:18:33.855 --> 00:18:38.175
And yet then getting the sort of
text of, of the problems you had,

00:18:38.235 --> 00:18:41.175
studying them on LingQ and going
back to it again the next day.

00:18:41.385 --> 00:18:42.945
Anyway, thank you for listening.

00:18:42.945 --> 00:18:44.054
I hope it wasn't too long.

00:18:44.445 --> 00:18:49.905
And, uh, I'll leave you with, uh, a
couple of videos where I, um, talk

00:18:49.905 --> 00:18:51.975
about Ukaraine and speak Ukrainian.

00:18:52.725 --> 00:18:53.565
Thanks for listening.

00:18:53.685 --> 00:18:54.185
Bye for now.

